{
 "id": "2022-mundher-historic",
 "name": "Historic Street Scenes: Visual Quality Rating and Element Tap (Baghdad)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: 32 photographs of Al-Rasheed Street, a historic street in Baghdad, Iraq, taken with a mobile camera about 50 m apart along the street from Hafiz Al-Qadhi Square to Al-Maydan Square. Participants answer the paper's seven demographic questions with its answer categories from Table 1 (gender, marital status, age group, educational level, living area, monthly income in Iraqi dinars, and how often they visited Al-Rashid Street during the previous year), then see the 32 photographs one at a time in random order, as in the paper. For each photograph they rate its visual quality on the paper's 5-point Likert scale from -2 (highly bad scene) to +2 (highly nice scene), and then tap the element inside the photograph that has an impact on their visual quality assessment, as in the paper's second subsection. Rebuilt from Mundher, Al-Sharaa, Al-Helli, Gao & Abu Bakar (2022), Visual Quality Assessment of Historical Street Scenes: A Case Study of the First \"Real\" Street Established in Baghdad, Heritage 5(4): 3680-3704, with the photographs from its Appendix A and the method from Section 2.3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: V01-V32 as coded in the paper; ratings are in quality and tap positions in element (x and y as fractions of the photograph's width and height from the top left), both keyed by image id; the paper's mean rating for each photograph is stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean visual quality per photograph, split into positive and negative scenes; a heatmap of tap density on each photograph to find the elements behind the ratings; and Mann-Whitney U tests of the ratings across demographic groups. The paper (225 completed responses from Baghdad, recruited on social media) found only 7 photographs rated positive (best V06, +0.48, and V07, +0.42) and 25 negative (worst V18, -1.29, and V12, -1.24); modern facade materials, security concrete walls and degraded facades lowered visual quality, while historical religious buildings, urban greenery and unique historical buildings raised it, and women and men rated the scenes differently. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper ran the survey on Qualtrics with a click heatmap question; here the tap is recorded as one point per photograph, from which a heatmap can be drawn; (2) the paper added two dummy photographs at the beginning and the end whose answers were discarded; they are not printed and are left out here; (3) the question wording, the labels of the three middle scale points and the instruction text are ours, because the paper describes them without printing them; the demographic questions use the paper's categories and, for the last one, its wording; (4) the paper limited participation to Baghdad; here anyone can take part; (5) the photographs are the appendix copies (about 455 x 256 px).",
